WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.540
These are the sounds of
 the mighty sperm whale.

00:00:03.826 --> 00:00:06.448
There's clear structure in how 
these animals communicate.

00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:09.061
We just don't yet know what they're&nbsp;saying.

00:00:09.061 --> 00:00:13.775
Our team of AI specialists, roboticists, 
linguists and marine biologists

00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:16.367
aim to use the&nbsp;most 
cutting-edge technologies

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:18.074
to make contact with another species,

00:00:18.074 --> 00:00:20.978
We're going to put specific&nbsp;
focus on the interactions

00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:22.461
of mothers and calfs,

00:00:22.461 --> 00:00:26.730
training our machine learning algorithms to&nbsp;
learn whale language from the bottom up.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.696
The raw audio and context data

00:00:28.696 --> 00:00:30.622
will go through our&nbsp;
machine learning engine

00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:32.811
where it's going to be first 
sorted by structure.

00:00:32.811 --> 00:00:34.868
The linguistics team&nbsp;will
 then search for things like

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.855
syntax and time displacement.

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:39.190
And once we've really mastered&nbsp;listening,

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.740
we're going to try really carefully

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.483
to talk back even on the 
most&nbsp;simplistic level.

00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:48.366
And all the platforms that we develop can be&nbsp;
cross-applied to other animals:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.366 --> 00:00:51.562
to elephants, birds, primates,&nbsp;dolphins --

00:00:51.562 --> 00:00:52.927
essentially any animal.

